{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Los Angeles Dodgers were a team defined by resilience and contradiction. They opened the year stumbling out of the gate — a 1-2 March gave way to an 11-14 April that featured early bullpen disasters, including Kenley Jansen's closer role being called into immediate question after a pair of blown saves in the season's first week. Wilmer Font and other depth arms were torched in blowout losses, and the team looked shakier than the defending NL champions had any right to look.\n\nThe turning point arrived quietly in June. A 17-9 month — the best stretch of the regular season — steadied the ship, anchored by clutch contributions from unexpected heroes. Kyle Farmer's walk-off double against the Cubs and Justin Turner's timely home run to answer the Mets captured the spirit of a team that could grind out wins even when the stars weren't shining brightest. July and August were uneven, plagued by more Jansen meltdowns and a brutal Ryan McMahon series in Colorado that threatened to derail the second half entirely, but the Dodgers never fully collapsed. The 92-71 record tells the story of a good team that kept finding ways.\n\nSeptember arrived like a vindication. Matt Kemp, the hometown hero returned, delivered consecutive big moments — a three-run homer on September 1st and a clutch double the very next day — as the Dodgers surged to an 18-9 mark and secured the NL West title with authority. The offense, powered by 235 home runs on the season and a .775 OPS, and the pitching staff's 3.38 ERA painted a portrait of a complete ballclub peaking at precisely the right time.\n\nThe postseason told a more complicated tale. David Freese's clutch single in the NLDS helped dispatch Atlanta, and the NLCS against Milwaukee brought heroics from Turner, Bellinger, and the Manny Machado acquisition. But the World Series against the Boston Red Sox — a series that featured a transcendent 18-inning Game 3 that ended on Max Muncy's walk-off home run — ultimately came up short. The Dodgers went to the very edge of glory, Yasiel Puig's defiant single and Pedro Báez's remarkable double play offering fleeting hope in the final innings, before Rafael Devers and the Red Sox closed the door. For the second straight year, Los Angeles came home without a championship ring, but not without a season worth remembering.",
